\par
EXPLANATORY NOTES FOR BS5500 AppG CALCULATION\par
=============================================\par
\par
                             NOZZLE SUMMARY\par
\par
                   Calculated : YES/NO\par
                   -------------------\par
There may be various reasons for which a specific nozzle or attachement
 needs not to be verified. In this case the option 'NO' appears.\par
\par
                   Method for the analysis\par
                   -----------------------\par
The procedures available include WRC107 for spheres and cylinders,
 WRC297, and BS5500 app.G.\par
\par
                   Summation convention\par
                   --------------------\par
This option concerns the manner to combine the individual components of
 the stress tensors connected with the pressure load and the external loads
 in order to obtain the complete status of stress around the attachment.\par
It is possible to use the WRC107 convention with the BS5500 analysis
 method, and viceversa.\par
\par
                   Shear reduced to foot\par
                   ---------------------\par
When this option is selected, the external forces and moments given
 in input are considered as applied to the nozzle end. Hence they are
 transferred autoamtically to the nozzle-to-shell junction by considering
 the nozzle height as the lever arm.\par
\par
                   External loads\par
                   --------------\par
The used specification for standard nozzle loads, if any, is quoted.\par
\par
                   Stress limits\par
                   -------------\par
If ASME is specified the local stresses are limited to 1.5 S{\sub m} (Div.2)
 and the secondary stresses are limited to 3 S{\sub m}.
 If BS5500 is specified, the corresponding limits (1.5f, 1.2f, 2.25f 2 f) are applied.\par
In all cases the limit on membrane compressive stress (0.9 S{\sub y}) dictaded by BS5500, App.G is apllied.\par
\par
                             1. DESIGN CONDITIONS\par
\par
                   Shell Material Design Stress\par
                   ----------------------------\par
If BS550 is specified above, the following values are both printed:\par
S{\sub m} :      Allowable according to ASME-VIII div.2\par
f  :      Allowable calculated according to BS5500 rules\par
These two values are used to establish the allowables for membrane
 and membrane plus bending stresses in the shell at the nozzle and at
 the pad edge (if any) in accordance to the code and supplementary
 requirements specified in the contract.\par
The obtained allowables are printed, for each nozzle and for each
 load condition, after item 12.\par
If ASME VIII is specified above, only S{\sub m} is displayed and utilised for the calculation of the stress limits.\par
\par
                             2. GEOMETRY\par
\par \page
                   Geometry of the Shell\par
                   ---------------------\par
Cones are treated as cylinders having a length equal to the generatrix
 of the cone, and a diameter equal to the diameter on which the attachment
 is installed, divided by the cosinus of the half apex angle.\par
Conoids are treated as cones obtained by revolving the generatrix
 of the conoid on which the attachment is installed.\par
Ellipsoidal and torispherical heads are treated as hemispherical
 heads having the same inside radius.\par
\par
                             3. GEOMETRIC PARAMETERS\par
\par
In general, the code symbols are used.\par
For circumferential and longitudinal moments, the symbols M.2, N.2
 are referred to the procedure G.2.3.3. When they are zero, the conditions
 for negligeability of G.2.2.2 are met.\par
For longitudinal moments the calculation of the stress indices is performed
 twice, as prescribed by G.2.3.3(a), but only the highest absolute
 resulting value is displayed, followed by a (-) or a (+), meaning if the
 maximum value occurs on the side of the attachment toward the centerline
 of the cylinder, or toward the cylinder end, respectively.\par
\par
                   INDIVIDUAL AND COMBINED STRESS COMPONENTS\par
A form similar to the App.G's suggested working form is used.\par
The numbers between parentheses on the right side refer to the row numbers
 of the App.G's working form.\par
For cylindrical shells, the local membrane stresses due to pressure
 (item 7) are calculated according to G.2.3.5.2(b). The local membrane
 plus bending stresses (item 8) are calculated according to G.2.3.5.2(a)
 at the nozzle, and according to (b) at the padge edge (if any).\par
\par
                             NOZZLE STRESSES SUMMARY\par
                   Factor k\par
                   --------\par
The factor multiplying all external load components, for which the stress limits
 are satisfied, is given. A factor less than one indicates that excessive external
 loads were specified.
\par
                   Nearest nozzles\par
                   ---------------\par
When this line appears, the program has been fed with a complete geometrical
 description of the equipment, which allows to evaluate the relative
 position of each couple of attachments installed on the same structural
 part. The nearest nozzle in the quadrant Qn is identified, if any.\par
 The definition of the quadrants is similar (rotate the quadrants 45{\super 0} round
 the nozzle axis) to Fig. G.2(21b). Quadrants Q1 and Q3 are in the
 longitudinal direction of the cylinder.\par
The item number of the nearest nozzle is followed by two keywords:\par
' NO  NO ' means that the influence of both radial load and moment is negligeable,
 according to the provisions of G.2.2.2. If it is not so, the
 corresponding stress components are added to those proper of the nozzle
 under consideration.\par

